1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,969
This place looks like another planet. Bright yellow and

2
00:00:03,969 --> 00:00:07,505
green pools. Ground made of salt. Air that smells

3
00:00:07,505 --> 00:00:10,825
like rotten eggs. Not far away, a volcano with

4
00:00:10,825 --> 00:00:13,928
a lake of boiling lava. But this isn't Mars

5
00:00:13,928 --> 00:00:17,464
or a moon of Jupiter. It's the Danakil Depression

6
00:00:17,464 --> 00:00:21,216
in Ethiopia, the hottest place on Earth where people

7
00:00:21,216 --> 00:00:24,897
actually live. And today, we'll see why you'd never

8
00:00:24,897 --> 00:00:25,835
survive here.

9
00:00:26,385 --> 00:00:29,837
Welcome to Earth Decoded. If you love the most

10
00:00:29,837 --> 00:00:33,814
extreme places on our planet, tap subscribe. Let's go

11
00:00:33,814 --> 00:00:34,939
to the Danakil.

12
00:00:35,489 --> 00:00:39,927
The Danakil Depression is in northeastern Ethiopia, near the

13
00:00:39,927 --> 00:00:43,698
border with Eritrea, in a region called Afar. Parts

14
00:00:43,698 --> 00:00:46,952
of it lie more than one hundred meters below

15
00:00:46,952 --> 00:00:47,692
sea level.

16
00:00:48,242 --> 00:00:51,233
Why is it so low? Because Africa is slowly

17
00:00:51,233 --> 00:00:55,150
tearing apart right here. Three tectonic plates meet in

18
00:00:55,150 --> 00:00:58,782
the Afar region, and they're pulling away from each

19
00:00:58,782 --> 00:01:02,129
other. As they separate, the land in the middle

20
00:01:02,129 --> 00:01:02,557
sinks.

21
00:01:03,107 --> 00:01:07,105
In 2005, scientists watched it happen. In just a

22
00:01:07,105 --> 00:01:11,436
few days, a crack about sixty kilometers long opened

23
00:01:11,436 --> 00:01:15,184
in the ground in Afar, in some places several

24
00:01:15,184 --> 00:01:16,184
meters wide.

25
00:01:16,734 --> 00:01:20,049
Long ago, the sea flooded this area. When the

26
00:01:20,049 --> 00:01:23,585
water was cut off and evaporated, it left behind

27
00:01:23,585 --> 00:01:27,783
enormous layers of salt. In millions of years, scientists

28
00:01:27,783 --> 00:01:30,877
think the Red Sea could flood back in, and

29
00:01:30,877 --> 00:01:33,161
a new ocean could be born here.

30
00:01:33,711 --> 00:01:37,280
Getting here is an adventure in itself. Most trips

31
00:01:37,280 --> 00:01:40,635
start in the highland city of Mekele, more than

32
00:01:40,635 --> 00:01:44,205
two thousand meters above sea level, where the air

33
00:01:44,205 --> 00:01:47,560
is pleasant and cool. Then the road drops down,

34
00:01:47,560 --> 00:01:50,701
and down, into the depression. In just a few

35
00:01:50,701 --> 00:01:53,628
hours, you go from mountain air to one of

36
00:01:53,628 --> 00:01:55,769
the hottest furnaces on Earth.

37
00:01:56,319 --> 00:01:59,340
Now the heat. The town of Dallol, at the

38
00:01:59,340 --> 00:02:02,965
north end of the depression, holds a record: the

39
00:02:02,965 --> 00:02:07,647
highest average annual temperature of any inhabited place ever

40
00:02:07,647 --> 00:02:12,178
measured. In the 1960s it averaged about thirty-four degrees

41
00:02:12,178 --> 00:02:16,181
Celsius. That's the average, day and night, all year.

42
00:02:16,731 --> 00:02:21,750
In summer, afternoon temperatures can climb above forty-five degrees.

43
00:02:21,750 --> 00:02:25,387
There's almost no shade, very little rain, and the

44
00:02:25,387 --> 00:02:28,805
salt flats reflect the sunlight back up at you.

45
00:02:29,355 --> 00:02:32,853
And night brings little relief. With an average of

46
00:02:32,853 --> 00:02:36,911
thirty-four degrees, even the nights are often hotter than

47
00:02:36,911 --> 00:02:40,409
a summer afternoon in many cities. Your body never

48
00:02:40,409 --> 00:02:43,627
really gets a chance to cool down and recover.

49
00:02:44,177 --> 00:02:47,774
Your body cools down by sweating. Here, you could

50
00:02:47,774 --> 00:02:50,930
lose more than a liter of sweat every hour.

51
00:02:50,930 --> 00:02:55,040
Without lots of water, dehydration and heat stroke could

52
00:02:55,040 --> 00:02:56,508
set in within hours.

53
00:02:57,058 --> 00:03:01,030
Quick question. What's the hottest temperature you've ever felt?

54
00:03:01,030 --> 00:03:02,519
Tell me in the comments.

55
00:03:03,069 --> 00:03:07,296
Then there's Dallol itself, a hydrothermal field on top

56
00:03:07,296 --> 00:03:10,984
of a buried volcano. Hot water rises through the

57
00:03:10,984 --> 00:03:15,287
salt, carrying minerals and gases from below, and builds

58
00:03:15,287 --> 00:03:19,129
strange shapes and pools in neon yellow, green and

59
00:03:19,129 --> 00:03:19,667
orange.

60
00:03:20,217 --> 00:03:24,438
Those beautiful colors come from sulfur, iron and salt.

61
00:03:24,438 --> 00:03:28,582
But the pools are extremely acidic. Some have measured

62
00:03:28,582 --> 00:03:32,112
below pH one, and some even below zero. That's

63
00:03:32,112 --> 00:03:35,949
more acidic than lemon juice, more acidic than the

64
00:03:35,949 --> 00:03:37,561
acid in your stomach.

65
00:03:38,111 --> 00:03:41,486
The water can also be scalding hot, near boiling.

66
00:03:41,486 --> 00:03:44,792
Touching it would burn your skin, and the ground

67
00:03:44,792 --> 00:03:47,754
around the pools is often a thin crust that

68
00:03:47,754 --> 00:03:49,545
can break under your feet.

69
00:03:50,095 --> 00:03:54,046
Nearby is a small pond called Gaet'ale, which formed

70
00:03:54,046 --> 00:03:57,692
after an earthquake in 2005. It's reported to be

71
00:03:57,692 --> 00:04:01,642
among the saltiest water on Earth, even saltier than

72
00:04:01,642 --> 00:04:04,985
the Dead Sea. Gas bubbles up through it, and

73
00:04:04,985 --> 00:04:08,783
the shore is littered with dead insects and birds.

74
00:04:09,333 --> 00:04:12,579
Around Dallol, you can also find the ruins of

75
00:04:12,579 --> 00:04:16,330
an old mining camp from the early twentieth century,

76
00:04:16,330 --> 00:04:20,225
when companies came to dig potash for fertilizer. Some

77
00:04:20,225 --> 00:04:23,614
of its buildings were made from blocks of salt.

78
00:04:23,614 --> 00:04:27,509
The heat and isolation eventually drove everyone away.

79
00:04:28,059 --> 00:04:32,540
Scientists study Dallol because it's so extreme. In 2019,

80
00:04:32,540 --> 00:04:36,391
a team reported that the hottest, most acidic and

81
00:04:36,391 --> 00:04:40,085
saltiest pools seemed to have no active life at

82
00:04:40,085 --> 00:04:43,937
all. On a planet where life is almost everywhere,

83
00:04:43,937 --> 00:04:45,745
that's incredibly rare.

84
00:04:46,295 --> 00:04:50,661
That's exactly why space scientists are interested. Places like

85
00:04:50,661 --> 00:04:54,266
Dallol help them understand where life can and can't

86
00:04:54,266 --> 00:04:57,662
survive, which matters when we search for life on

87
00:04:57,662 --> 00:05:00,850
Mars or other worlds. If something can live in

88
00:05:00,850 --> 00:05:03,692
the Danakil, it might be able to live out

89
00:05:03,692 --> 00:05:04,385
there too.

90
00:05:04,935 --> 00:05:08,137
And it's not only the water. Some spots in

91
00:05:08,137 --> 00:05:12,406
the Danakil release toxic gases, like carbon dioxide and

92
00:05:12,406 --> 00:05:16,827
hydrogen sulfide, that collect in low places where there's

93
00:05:16,827 --> 00:05:17,437
no wind.

94
00:05:17,987 --> 00:05:21,524
Visitors have found dead birds and insects next to

95
00:05:21,524 --> 00:05:24,849
some pools, likely killed by the gas. You might

96
00:05:24,849 --> 00:05:27,466
not see it or smell it in time, which

97
00:05:27,466 --> 00:05:30,508
makes it one of the most dangerous parts of

98
00:05:30,508 --> 00:05:31,640
the whole place.

99
00:05:32,190 --> 00:05:35,064
Would you visit the Danakil with a guide, or

100
00:05:35,064 --> 00:05:37,677
is this one place you'd skip? Tell me in

101
00:05:37,677 --> 00:05:38,526
the comments.

102
00:05:39,076 --> 00:05:42,833
South of Dallol stands Erta Ale, a volcano whose

103
00:05:42,833 --> 00:05:47,137
name means smoking mountain in the local Afar language.

104
00:05:47,137 --> 00:05:50,893
For decades, it held something very rare: a lake

105
00:05:50,893 --> 00:05:53,476
of liquid lava inside its crater.

106
00:05:54,026 --> 00:05:57,365
Only a handful of volcanoes on Earth have had

107
00:05:57,365 --> 00:06:01,223
long-lasting lava lakes. At Erta Ale, the lava glows

108
00:06:01,223 --> 00:06:04,858
at more than one thousand degrees Celsius, and at

109
00:06:04,858 --> 00:06:07,381
night the whole crater shines red.

110
00:06:07,931 --> 00:06:11,419
Climbing it isn't easy. Most visitors start the hike

111
00:06:11,419 --> 00:06:14,506
in the evening, when it's a little cooler, and

112
00:06:14,506 --> 00:06:17,659
walk for hours across sharp, black lava rock in

113
00:06:17,659 --> 00:06:20,342
the dark, just to reach the rim and look

114
00:06:20,342 --> 00:06:22,288
down into the glowing crater.

115
00:06:22,838 --> 00:06:26,389
The lava lake changes over the years. It rises,

116
00:06:26,389 --> 00:06:30,620
overflows, drains away and returns. Visitors who hike up

117
00:06:30,620 --> 00:06:34,095
at night often camp near the rim, close enough

118
00:06:34,095 --> 00:06:36,513
to feel the heat on their faces.

119
00:06:37,063 --> 00:06:39,940
And yet, people live and work here. The Afar

120
00:06:39,940 --> 00:06:43,341
people have lived in this region for centuries. Some

121
00:06:43,341 --> 00:06:45,956
of them work as salt miners on the flats

122
00:06:45,956 --> 00:06:47,068
near Lake Assale.

123
00:06:47,618 --> 00:06:50,593
They pry up the salt crust with sticks and

124
00:06:50,593 --> 00:06:53,498
axes and cut it into neat blocks by hand,

125
00:06:53,498 --> 00:06:57,111
in that same brutal heat. Then camel caravans carry

126
00:06:57,111 --> 00:07:00,653
the blocks out of the depression, a tradition that

127
00:07:00,653 --> 00:07:02,637
goes back hundreds of years.

128
00:07:03,187 --> 00:07:06,637
Salt from the Danakil was once so valuable that

129
00:07:06,637 --> 00:07:09,866
bars of it, called amole, were used as money

130
00:07:09,866 --> 00:07:13,830
in parts of Ethiopia, well into the twentieth century.

131
00:07:13,830 --> 00:07:16,179
People literally paid with salt.

132
00:07:16,729 --> 00:07:19,928
They survive because they know this land: when to

133
00:07:19,928 --> 00:07:22,605
work, where to find water and how to rest

134
00:07:22,605 --> 00:07:26,261
through the worst heat. A visitor without that knowledge

135
00:07:26,261 --> 00:07:27,502
wouldn't last long.

136
00:07:28,052 --> 00:07:31,630
Not far from here, in the wider Afar region,

137
00:07:31,630 --> 00:07:35,778
scientists found the famous fossil Lucy in 1974, an

138
00:07:35,778 --> 00:07:40,007
early human relative who lived about three point two

139
00:07:40,007 --> 00:07:43,667
million years ago. This land has been part of

140
00:07:43,667 --> 00:07:46,676
the human story for a very long time.

141
00:07:47,226 --> 00:07:50,731
So, could you survive the Danakil on your own?

142
00:07:50,731 --> 00:07:55,150
Heat above forty-five degrees. Almost no fresh water. Acid

143
00:07:55,150 --> 00:07:58,883
pools, toxic gas, fragile ground and a lava lake.

144
00:07:58,883 --> 00:08:02,693
Most visitors only go with local guides, plenty of

145
00:08:02,693 --> 00:08:07,112
water and vehicles, and many check travel advisories first

146
00:08:07,112 --> 00:08:09,626
because the region can be unsafe.

147
00:08:10,176 --> 00:08:13,389
It's one of the harshest places on our planet.

148
00:08:13,389 --> 00:08:16,323
But it's also one of the most beautiful, a

149
00:08:16,323 --> 00:08:20,095
window into how continents split, how oceans are born,

150
00:08:20,095 --> 00:08:23,168
and how far life, and people, can be pushed.

151
00:08:23,718 --> 00:08:27,596
Last question. Which extreme place should we explore next?

152
00:08:27,596 --> 00:08:29,201
Tell me in the comments.

153
00:08:29,751 --> 00:08:33,475
Thanks for watching Earth Decoded. If you enjoyed this,

154
00:08:33,475 --> 00:08:36,589
like the video, subscribe, and share it with a

155
00:08:36,589 --> 00:08:39,772
friend who complains about the heat. See you in

156
00:08:39,772 --> 00:08:40,652
the next one.

